                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-SEC-001] Jupyter kernel HMAC verification is a no-op stub; all inbound messages accepted -- verify_message() returns Ok(()) unconditionally: after the key-empty early return it computes frames_for_signing() then discards them with \u0027let _ = frames;\u0027 and returns Ok(()) without ever calling hmac::verify. The on-wire hmac_hex frame is discarded by parse_message() (lines 271-312) so it is not even available. The working verifier verify_message_with_signature() is marked #[allow(dead_code)] (line 338) and never invoked. Main loop line 225 calls the stub, so unsigned/forged execute_request messages are compiled and executed (rustc spawn + binary execution), violating the Jupyter spec requirement that unverified messages be dropped."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-security",
                                         "level":  "error",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buff-jupyter/src/kernel.rs"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  316,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  334
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-SEC-002] Kernel binds sockets to arbitrary connection-file IP; no loopback restriction or warning -- ConnectionFile::validate() only checks transport==\u0027tcp\u0027 and signature_scheme; the ip field is never validated and the field doc (line 44) explicitly blesses \u0027*\u0027 and \u00270.0.0.0\u0027. ZmqSocketSet::bind (transport.rs:123-153) binds shell/iopub/control/hb sockets to exactly that endpoint. A connection file with ip=\u00270.0.0.0\u0027 exposes an execute-capable kernel to the network with no warning. Combined with AUD-SEC-001 (HMAC never enforced) this yields unauthenticated remote code execution; even with HMAC fixed, unsigned mode (empty key+scheme) is also accepted by validate() (test line 219-233)."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-security",
                                         "level":  "warning",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buff-jupyter/src/connection.rs"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  123,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  137
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-SEC-003] buffup verifies downloads with checksum from the same origin as the artifact (no signature) -- fetch_checksum_sidecar() downloads \u0027\u003ctarball_url\u003e.sha256\u0027 from the same GitHub host serving the tarball (line 122: format!(\"{tarball_url}.sha256\")); install.rs:78-84 compares SHA-256. This defends against corruption only: any adversary able to substitute the tarball (compromised release workflow, GitHub account, malicious redirect of both URLs) can equally substitute the sidecar. The installed artifact is an executable placed on PATH, so the trust requirement is integrity against an active adversary, which requires an independent signing key (rustup uses GPG; Homebrew uses its own seal). --skip-checksum is documented and warned, so the flag itself is not a finding."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-security",
                                         "level":  "warning",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buffup/src/github.rs"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  118,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  140
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-SEC-004] Registry bearer and session tokens stored unhashed at rest in SQLite -- add_token() inserts the raw bearer token (\u0027INSERT OR IGNORE INTO tokens (token) VALUES (?1)\u0027, line 211) and session creation stores raw session tokens in the sessions table (line 572). validate_token() then compares by SQL string equality (lines 396-408); InMemoryStorage uses HashSet::contains (storage.rs:587-593). Any read of registry.db (backup leak, path traversal elsewhere, incident response artifact) yields live publish credentials. crates.io stores only SHA-256 digests of tokens for exactly this reason. The non-constant-time comparison is low practical risk (network timing over SQL/B-tree and Rust\u0027s SipHash seeding) and is noted, not the core issue."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-security",
                                         "level":  "note",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buff-registry/src/storage_sqlite.rs"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  208,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  216
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-SEC-005] buff login writes ~/.buff/credentials without restrictive permissions -- save_credentials_to() creates parent dirs and writes the TOML containing the bearer token with plain fs::write (line 238). On Unix with default umask 022 the file lands 0644, readable by every local user on shared/CI hosts; no 0600 chmod (Unix) or restrictive ACL (Windows) is applied, unlike `cargo login` which sets 0600. The doc comment only acknowledges missing atomic write (\u0027temp-file + rename\u0027 future hardening, line 224) and is silent on permissions, so this is not a documented acceptance."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-security",
                                         "level":  "note",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buff-lang-cli/src/commands/registry.rs"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  231,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  241
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-SEC-006] Publish endpoints store dependency names without validation, bypassing the P0.28 layered defense -- Both publish (request.deps used at line 418 has_cycle and stored at lines 447-455) and multipart_publish (meta.deps stored at lines 820-829) accept DepSpec.name strings with no validate_no_path_traversal/validate_package_name call, even though the P0.28 header (lines 56-65) states the traversal guard \u0027runs on EVERY user-supplied path-y input\u0027. A dep name like \u0027../evil\u0027 or \u0027@org/pkg/extra\u0027 is persisted and later echoed in package metadata and walked by has_cycle. Today dep names never reach the filesystem (tarball paths use validated name+version), so there is no direct exploit; this is a defense-in-depth gap against the crate\u0027s own stated invariant and future storage backends that might key on dep names."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-security",
                                         "level":  "note",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buff-registry/src/handlers.rs"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  446,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  455
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-TC-001] 8 of 37 CLI subcommands have zero test coverage (gen, repl, expand, jupyter, bench_compile, bench_program, extract_function, inline_variable) -- The CLI defines 37+ subcommand variants in cli.rs (Build/Run/New/Gen/.../Rename/ExtractFunction/InlineVariable). A case-insensitive name-mention scan (CamelCase + snake_case + squashed forms) of all 40 files in crates/buff-lang-cli/tests found zero references to Gen, Repl, Expand, Jupyter, BenchCompile, BenchProgram, ExtractFunction, and InlineVariable. The buff-jupyter and buff-repl crates have their own unit tests, but the CLI dispatch layer for `buff jupyter install`, `buff repl`, `buff gen`, `buff expand`, and the extract/inline refactor operations is never driven by any test. refactor_tests.rs covers only the `rename` operation."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-test-coverage",
                                         "level":  "error",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
                                                                                                                 "uri":  "crates/buff-lang-cli/tests"
                                                                                                             },
                                                                                        "region":  {
                                                                                                       "startLine":  1,
                                                                                                       "endLine":  1
                                                                                                   }
                                                                                    }
                                                           }
                                                       ]
                                     },
                                     {
                                         "message":  {
                                                         "text":  "[AUD-TC-002] Golden fixture gate exercises only 5 of 62 stable ErrorCodes; E14xx runtime family has zero fixtures -- code.rs defines 62 stable ErrorCode variants (E1001-E1014 lex, E1101-E1110 parse, E1201-E1214 type, E1301-E1304 codegen, E1401-E1410 runtime, E1501-E1510 lint) that are promised stable forever. The dedicated fixture-coverage gate (error_code_coverage.rs, 178 lines) asserts exactly 5 codes: E1001, E1102, E1202, E1301, E1501. tests/fixtures/invalid/ holds 11 .buff fixtures for a 62-code surface. A workspace-wide scan of all crates/*/tests/*.rs found only 13 distinct ErrorCode variants asserted anywhere in integration tests; the entire E14xx runtime family (GpuDispatchFailed, ShaderCompileFailed, ChannelClosed, etc. - 10 codes) and 9 of 14 lex codes (E1004-E1014) have no fixture and no integration assertion. The file\u0027s own doc table lists 6 fixtures where one exists only as documentation."
                                                     },
                                         "ruleId":  "BUFF-test-coverage",
                                         "level":  "error",
                                         "locations":  [
                                                           {
                                                               "physicalLocation":  {
                                                                                        "artifactLocation":  {
